{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPiwrG9taag5th4eemDL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't give you that fact sheet yet, and I want to be direct about why rather than hand you something that looks finished.\n\n**I have no source access in this session.** WebSearch and WebFetch both came back \"permission not granted,\" there are no documents in the working folder, and nothing you've given me to work from. So I cannot retrieve a single citation. Writing author/title/publication/year from memory is precisely how fabricated-but-plausible citations get into print — correct format is not evidence the source exists — and this is running under your name, as-is, tonight. I'm not doing it.\n\n**What unblocks this in about ten minutes:** approve the web tool permission prompt and I'll run the verification pass immediately — each citation checked individually, primary sources where they exist, and a plain statement of what I could and couldn't confirm.\n\nIn the meantime, here's the honest version of what I have — **a worksheet, not a fact sheet.** Nothing below is verified. Don't print any of it.\n\n### Bibliographic leads to confirm (NOT citations)\n\n- Ward's own monograph, *On the Growth of Plants in Closely Glazed Cases* — London, John Van Voorst, I believe 1842, with a second edition around 1852. Need to confirm both editions, publisher, and exact year.\n- Luke Keogh, *The Wardian Case: How a Simple Box Moved Plants and Changed the World* — University of Chicago Press, I believe 2020. This is the modern scholarly treatment and likely your best single source. Confirm year and subtitle.\n- David Elliston Allen's work on the Victorian fern craze — late 1960s. Confirm title, publisher, year.\n\nTreat all three as \"probably real, verify before citing.\"\n\n### The three claims that need the most care\n\n**The 1833 Sydney voyage.** I have partial recall of ship names and an 1833 arrival with a return voyage reaching London in 1834, and my confidence is low enough that I won't repeat the specifics here — you'd anchor a dated claim to them. These need primary sourcing: Ward's own account, or Keogh, who works from archival shipping records.\n\n**The survival rates.** The widely circulated figure is roughly one in twenty plants surviving long sea transport before, and near-total survival after. Two problems worth knowing even once it's sourced:\n\n1. Provenance is murky. It's repeated constantly, often without attribution, and appears to trace back to contemporaneous estimates by Ward and his circle — people with a direct interest in the case succeeding.\n2. It isn't a measured survival rate from any study. It's an anecdotal era-estimate. If your piece frames it as data, that framing will be the weakest point in the article, and it's the sort of thing a sharp editor or reader catches.\n\nMy recommendation regardless of what verification turns up: attribute it in-text (\"Ward himself estimated…\") rather than presenting it as an established statistic.\n\n**\"Changed plant transport survival.\"** The directional claim is solid and uncontroversial. The precise magnitude is the soft part.\n\n---\n\nGrant the web permission and I'll turn this into the publishable version with real citations, plus a short note on which claims are firm and which need hedging. If you'd rather not enable it, tell me and I'll instead give you a structured research checklist — exact questions, and which source answers each — so you or a fact-checker can close it out directly."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":660,"cache_read_input_tokens":34682,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":660},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b4895c